# Issue 14 - Configure Twingate

## What was found

`TWINGATE_API_TOKEN`, `TWINGATE_ACCESS_TOKEN`, `TWINGATE_REFRESH_TOKEN` and
`TWINGATE_NETWORK` are present as repository secrets. The Admin API token
worked, but the Twingate tenant had no Remote Network, Connector or Resource.
That is why the connector tokens could not produce a working private endpoint.

## What was provisioned

All objects were created with the Admin API through the new
**Twingate Admin** workflow. No token value was printed, written to the
repository or committed.

| Object | Value |
| --- | --- |
| Tenant | Resolved from `TWINGATE_NETWORK` (same tenant as the API token) |
| Remote Network | `Ultron SMP` - id `UmVtb3RlTmV0d29yazo0NjQ5` |
| Connector | `twingate-mccon` - id `Q29ubmVjdG9yOjEwNDIz` |
| Resource | `minecraft` - id `UmVzb3VyY2U6OTk1OA==` |
| Resource address | `127.0.0.1:25565`, TCP restricted to `25565`, UDP/ICMP disabled |
| Access | `Everyone` group assigned to the `minecraft` resource |

A fresh Connector access/refresh token pair was generated and written directly
to the `TWINGATE_ACCESS_TOKEN` / `TWINGATE_REFRESH_TOKEN` repository secrets
through `gh secret set`. The values exist only in GitHub Secrets and the
mode-600 temporary file that was deleted at the end of the workflow run.

## Verification runs

| Run | Result |
| --- | --- |
| Twingate Admin `44309254877` (status) | Confirmed the tenant was empty |
| Twingate Admin `44309354552` (provision + rotate tokens) | Created the Remote Network, Connector, token pair and resource |
| Verify Twingate Connector `44309522392` | Connector container healthy, log `State: Online`, API state `ALIVE` |
| Twingate Admin `44310470316` | Idempotent provisioning; assigned `Everyone` to the resource |
| Run UltronSMP `44310694698` | `start_twingate_connector` and `ensure_twingate_resource` passed; run cancelled after validation |
| Twingate Admin `44311505431` (final status) | Resource shows `groups=Everyone`; connector is `DEAD_HEARTBEAT_TOO_OLD` because the verification runner has stopped |

`Run UltronSMP` was deliberately cancelled after the Twingate steps so the
long-lived server loop did not stay online. The Twingate steps themselves
completed successfully before cancellation.

## Code changes

- `scripts/twingate-admin.py`
  - `status` lists Remote Networks, Connectors, Groups and Resources with
    their access groups.
  - `provision` creates a missing Remote Network/Connector, can generate a
    new Connector token pair into a mode-600 file, and is idempotent.
- `.github/workflows/twingate-admin.yml`
  - Manual status/provision workflow. `rotate_tokens=true` also updates the
    connector secrets with `gh secret set`.
- `.github/workflows/twingate-verify.yml`
  - Starts the connector on a runner, waits for health, prints the account
    state and connector log, then stops the connector.
- `scripts/twingate-resource.py`
  - Resolves `TWINGATE_RESOURCE_GROUP_NAMES` and assigns those groups when
    creating or updating the `minecraft` resource.
- `.github/workflows/run.yml`
  - Passes `TWINGATE_RESOURCE_GROUP_NAMES=Everyone` (overridable by a
    repository variable) so the resource keeps its access assignment.
- `.github/workflows/twingate-resource.yml`
  - Same group default for manual resource runs.
- `README.md` and `.agent-work/tests/`
  - Documented the admin/verify workflows and added admin/resource group tests.

## Local tests

Passed:

```bash
bash .agent-work/tests/test_twingate_connector.sh
bash .agent-work/tests/test_twingate_resource.sh
bash .agent-work/tests/test_twingate_admin.sh
bash .agent-work/tests/test_run.sh
python3 -m py_compile scripts/twingate-resource.py scripts/twingate-admin.py
```

## Remaining notes

- Twingate is a private-access service, not a public tunnel. The connector is
  online only while `run.yml` is running on the ephemeral GitHub runner. It
  therefore shows `DEAD_HEARTBEAT_TOO_OLD` whenever no run is active; start
  `run.yml` and it returns to `ALIVE` (verified in run `44309522392`).
- Each user who needs access must have the Twingate client installed and be a
  member of the `Everyone` group (or another group added to the resource).
- The public Minecraft tunnel is unchanged and remains the path for players
  who do not use Twingate.
====ISSUE13====
# Issue 13 - Use the Twingate connector command

## Request

Issue 13 supplies the Docker command Twingate prints for its connector and asks
for it to be used. The command includes:

- `twingate/connector:1`
- `--sysctl net.ipv4.ping_group_range="0 2147483647"`
- `TWINGATE_NETWORK=mikailamin`
- `TWINGATE_LABEL_HOSTNAME=ultronsmp.net`
- `TWINGATE_LABEL_DEPLOYED_BY=docker`
- container name `twingate-mccon`
- `--restart=unless-stopped`
- `--pull=always`

The token values in the issue body are empty, so the implementation continues
to read them from `TWINGATE_ACCESS_TOKEN` / `TWINGATE_REFRESH_TOKEN` only. No
token was pasted into the repository or logs. The backticks around
`ultronsmp.net` in the issue body were treated as a copy/paste artifact; the
label is the literal value `ultronsmp.net`.

## Changes

- `scripts/twingate-connector.sh`
  - Defaults now match the issue: container `twingate-mccon`, network
    `mikailamin` fallback from the workflow, `--pull always`, hostname/deployed
    labels, and `--restart unless-stopped`.
  - Added `TWINGATE_DOCKER_PULL`, `TWINGATE_LABEL_HOSTNAME` and
    `TWINGATE_LABEL_DEPLOYED_BY` overrides.
  - Added `TWINGATE_DOCKER_NETWORK=default`, which omits `--network` so the
    connector uses Docker's default bridge network exactly like the issue
    command, while still adding the recommended sysctl.
  - Kept host networking as the repository default because the `minecraft`
    Twingate Resource points at `127.0.0.1:25565` on the runner. Docker rejects
    the ping_group_range sysctl in the host namespace, so it is still omitted
    for host mode.
- `.github/workflows/run.yml`
  - Uses `TWINGATE_NETWORK=mikailamin` as the fallback after repository
    variable/secret overrides.
  - Sets `twingate-mccon`, `--pull always`, hostname `ultronsmp.net` and
    deployed-by `docker` defaults, all overridable with repository variables.
- `.github/workflows/twingate-resource.yml`
  - Uses the same `mikailamin` network fallback so the Resource and Connector
    target the same tenant.
- `README.md`
  - Documents the new defaults, overrides and `default` network mode.
- `.agent-work/tests/test_twingate_connector.sh`
  - Fake Docker now records pull policy and labels and asserts the new
    defaults.
  - Added coverage for `TWINGATE_DOCKER_NETWORK=default` (no `--network`,
    sysctl present).

## Validation

Commands run successfully:

```bash
bash -n scripts/twingate-connector.sh
bash -n .agent-work/tests/test_twingate_connector.sh
shellcheck scripts/twingate-connector.sh
shellcheck .agent-work/tests/test_twingate_connector.sh
python3 -c 'import yaml; yaml.safe_load(open(".github/workflows/run.yml")); yaml.safe_load(open(".github/workflows/twingate-resource.yml"))'
bash .agent-work/tests/test_twingate_connector.sh
bash .agent-work/tests/test_twingate_docker_host.sh
```

The real-Docker test confirmed the host-network sysctl conflict still exists
and that `twingate/connector:1` starts under host networking without the
sysctl. It used fake credentials only and reported `Authentication` / `Error`,
which is expected without real tokens.

## Limitation

An end-to-end Twingate connection still requires the real connector token
pair and the Admin Console Resource assignment. Those secrets were not
available to this run and were not required to validate the command wiring.
====ISSUE11====
# Issue 11 - Fix the Twingate connector startup failure

## Root cause

The `start_twingate_connector` step in `.github/workflows/run.yml` failed with:

```text
docker: Error response from daemon: failed to create task for container:
failed to create shim task: OCI runtime create failed: runc create failed:
sysctl "net.ipv4.ping_group_range" not allowed in host network namespace
```

`scripts/twingate-connector.sh` started the connector with both
`--network host` and `--sysctl net.ipv4.ping_group_range="0 2147483647"`.
Docker rejects that sysctl when the container shares the host network
namespace, so the connector container never started. Because the workflow
step uses `continue-on-error: true`, the failure appeared as a warning while
the rest of the server run continued.

## Changes

- `scripts/twingate-connector.sh`
  - Added `TWINGATE_DOCKER_NETWORK` (default `host`).
  - Applies the Twingate-recommended `net.ipv4.ping_group_range` sysctl only
    for non-host network modes.
  - Host networking is kept so the connector can reach the Minecraft server at
    `127.0.0.1:25565` on the runner.
- `.agent-work/tests/test_twingate_connector.sh`
  - Fake Docker now reproduces the real host-netns sysctl restriction.
  - Asserts that host mode does not pass the sysctl.
  - Adds a bridge-mode case that asserts the sysctl is still passed.
- `.agent-work/tests/test_twingate_docker_host.sh`
  - Real-Docker regression test. It reproduces the exact failure and verifies
    that `twingate/connector:1` starts with host networking when the sysctl is
    omitted. Uses generated fake credentials only.
- `README.md`
  - Documents the host-network behaviour and the optional
    `TWINGATE_DOCKER_NETWORK` override.

## Validation

Passed:

```bash
bash -n scripts/twingate-connector.sh
bash -n .agent-work/tests/test_twingate_connector.sh
python3 -c 'import yaml; yaml.safe_load(open(".github/workflows/run.yml"))'
bash .agent-work/tests/test_twingate_connector.sh
bash .agent-work/tests/test_twingate_docker_host.sh
```

The real-Docker test reproduced the original error and then showed the
connector image starting successfully without the sysctl. With fake
credentials the connector reports `State: Authentication` / `State: Error`,
as expected; a real token pair is still required for an end-to-end tunnel.

Live verification in the actual workflow:

